	Mechanical Engineering and Structural Design of the Containers
Translating these biomaterials into functional packaging requires rethinking conventional cosmetic mechanics. Standard petro-plastic assemblies rely on dissimilar polymer tribology—pairing Polyoxymethylene (POM) spirals against ABS cam sleeves and PP elevator cups—to prevent galling and friction lock, while incorporating metal coil springs or zamac weights for haptics38. Because mixed-material assemblies cannot be recycled or composted without manual destruction, a biocompostable container must utilize mono-material mechanical engineering9.
Engineering the Refillable Lipstick Mechanism
In a split-lifecycle lipstick, the reusable base houses the actuation drive, while the refill cartridge consists of the solid wax-oil bullet pre-cast or inserted into a coated bamboo-bagasse pulp sleeve6. Three distinct mechanical drive architectures can be executed using mono-material PHA (specifically nucleated PHBV) and Sulapac outer housings.
The first approach is a mono-material three-piece cam-and-spiral swivel that retains the classic rotational gesture5. In this configuration, the inner slotted body, the helically grooved cam sleeve (A-shell), and the central elevator cup (godet) are all injection-molded from nucleated PHBV9. To prevent friction welding or stick-slip chattering when two identical biopolymers rotate against one another, the mold tooling for the helical cam track is textured with a micro-matte EDM finish while the elevator lugs are polished to a mirror finish, reducing the interfacial contact area9. Incorporating    bio-based fatty acid amide slip agents into the PHBV masterbatch further stabilizes the operating torque across the entire stroke10.
A second, mechanically simpler approach utilizes a two-part self-threading conveying drive that eliminates the thin-walled spiral sleeve entirely14. This mono-material mechanism employs a stationary central screw spindle molded directly into the twist base14. When the consumer inserts a hexagonally cross-sectioned solid refill cartridge and twists the base, the internal thread cuts directly into the base of the product or its molded carrier, driving the bullet forward and backward with zero auxiliary pins, cams, or springs14. The hexagonal inner profile of the outer barrel acts as a geometric anti-rotation keyway that prevents the bullet from spinning during actuation while simultaneously increasing shipping packing density14.
A third option replaces rotational kinematics altogether with a linear slider mechanism41. Because semi-crystalline biopolymers can exhibit slight dimensional drift under fluctuating humidity, helical twist tracks with tight radial clearances (  ) risk binding if improperly conditioned12. Adopting a patented thumb-slider actuator—as demonstrated in the Toujours Lipstick or bottom-loading Glide Compact—eliminates helical friction paths by guiding a single PHA elevator shuttle along a straight axial slot inside the Sulapac outer case, engaging detents at the top and bottom of the stroke41.
The Refill Locking Interface and Torque Specifications
Regardless of the chosen drive kinematics, the mechanical junction where the home-compostable refill cartridge meets the reusable elevator mechanism represents the most critical tolerance zone in the assembly44. In the award-winning Maya system developed by Morrama and PPK Packaging, the bamboo-bagasse refill cartridge features a removable pulp hood that shields the bullet during transit and slides smoothly into the reusable case with a simple twist6. The base of the refill cartridge locks into the reusable actuator via an annular snap-fit—comprising cantilevered PHA snap lugs engaging snap slots on the refill collar—or a two-lug bayonet fitting44.
To ensure the consumer can swap refills effortlessly without crushing the pulp cylinder or accidentally dislodging the refill when uncapping the lipstick, the mechanical retention forces must be strictly tiered. The cap pull-off release force, engineered via a precision interference rib inside the Sulapac Luxe cap, is tuned to require    of axial pull force accompanied by a crisp acoustic "click"9. Conversely, the refill cartridge retention force securing the refill sleeve into the reusable base must exceed the cap pull-off force by at least a factor of two—requiring    of axial extraction force, or an orthogonal counter-rotation release gesture in a bayonet mount or push-and-pull reload system—so routine uncapping never unseats the cartridge42. Finally, validated on an automated motorized torque tester such as a Mecmesin Vortex-xt, the rotational operating and release torque required to twist the lipstick barrel up and down must remain smoothly within    (  ), free of spike anomalies that signal mold flash or helical binding50.
Engineering the Reusable Cosmetic Tub and Jar
For skincare creams, balms, and loose powders, the container utilizes a double-walled pod architecture consisting of four primary components: a heavy-walled outer vessel, a threaded outer closure, a replaceable inner refill cup, and a hermetic sealing element5. The reusable outer jar (molded at    wall thickness from Sulapac Solid 2.0 or Premium) and the matching lid (molded from Sulapac Luxe Flex) are machined to standardized Glass Packaging Institute (GPI) continuous thread specifications, such as the beauty industry benchmark 53-400 neck finish for wide-mouth    (  ) containers4. However, because wood-filled biocomposites exhibit higher notch sensitivity than virgin PP (with notched Charpy impact strength of    versus unnotched impact strength of   )10, sharp V-threads must be avoided. Specifying a modified buttress or rounded-knuckle thread profile distributes axial clamping loads evenly and provides the high-torque resistance necessary to prevent thread stripping9.
The replaceable inner refill cup (   wall thickness, molded from Sulapac Barrier or thermoformed coated bagasse pulp) features an upper seating flange and an undercut retention bead around its circumference4. Pressing the refill pod into the outer jar deflects the rim slightly to produce an audible "click" when closed, locking the pod against rotation when the consumer unscrews the outer cap14. To replace the non-recyclable foamed Polyethylene (PE) cap liner used in conventional jars, two microplastic-free hermetic sealing architectures can be deployed. First, a compressible ring of P4HB elastomer (   elongation) can be two-shot overmolded or press-fitted into an undercut channel inside the Sulapac lid; when the 53-400 cap is torqued down, the P4HB gasket compresses against the top rim of the inner refill cup to form an airtight seal5. Second, as pioneered by YOM Beauty for The Pebble lip care vessel, both the inner seal disk and integrated application spatula can be precision-molded directly from Sulapac grades, creating a    wood-biocomposite friction plug seal27.
Barrier Coating Engineering for Formula Compatibility
The single greatest technical vulnerability of bio-based primary refills—especially molded bamboo/bagasse pulp and thin-walled biopolymers—is their permeability to water vapor, oxygen, and low-molecular-weight cosmetic oils1. Uncoated molded pulp is inherently hydrophilic and porous; it rapidly wicks liquid esters and castor oil out of a lipstick bullet (leaving the product dry, brittle, and prone to oxidative rancidity) while losing structural stiffness in humid bathrooms1. Similarly, if a water-in-oil skincare emulsion loses more than    of its water mass over a 12-week accelerated aging test at   , the emulsion phase-separates4. To achieve commercial cosmetic shelf life (12 to 36 months) without fluorochemicals (PFAS) or polyethylene laminations, manufacturers apply one of three biocompostable barrier technologies to the interior of the refill cartridge58.
Modified Nanocellulose (CNF and CNC) Multilayer Coatings
Cellulose nanofibrils (CNF) and cellulose nanocrystals (CNC) derived from wood or agricultural waste (such as fique or sugarcane bagasse) form dense, hydrogen-bonded crystalline networks when cast or sprayed onto porous pulp substrates35. Produced via TEMPO-mediated oxidation (introducing    of anionic    carboxyl groups at the C6 glucose position at energy inputs of   ) or carboxymethylation (degree of substitution    at   ), followed by 3 to 5 passes through a high-pressure homogenizer at   , the resulting nanofibrils measure    in diameter and    in length with specific surface areas of   35.
Because neither non-polar oxygen gas nor hydrophobic cosmetic oils and greases interact with or disrupt the tight hydrogen-bonded crystalline lattice of nanocellulose, a thin    (  ) CNF coating delivers an exceptional lipid and oxygen barrier that rivals synthetic Ethylene Vinyl Alcohol (EVOH) and aluminum oxide films35. However, pristine nanocellulose is sensitive to moisture; high relative humidity swells the fibrils and degrades the Water Vapor Transmission Rate (WVTR)36.
