High-Barrier Bio-Polyesters (Polyglycolic Acid and Sulapac Barrier)
When dispersion-coating pulp is insufficient for aggressive water-based skincare serums, the refill pod can be co-extruded or lined with Polyglycolic Acid (PGA)58. Structurally analogous to PLA but lacking the pendant methyl side group, PGA packs into a highly dense crystalline structure that achieves a moisture permeability of    and an oxygen permeability of   —orders of magnitude tighter than semi-crystalline PBS (  ) or PCL/PEG blends58. Alternatively, injection-molding the inner cup directly from Sulapac Barrier eliminates secondary coating steps altogether, holding emulsion moisture loss to    over 12 weeks at   4.
Atomic-Scale PECVD Silica Bilayer and Trilayer Deposition
For premium skincare, sunscreens, or volatile active formulations—such as retinoids, antioxidants, or Anjac Group's high-water AquaStick solid emulsions8—where zero gas permeation and zero flavor or fragrance scalping are mandatory, Plasma-Enhanced Chemical Vapor Deposition (PECVD) provides an invisible glass barrier inside the molded biopolymer or pulp refill cup61. Depositing brittle inorganic silica (  ) directly onto a relatively flexible biodegradable polyester (like PHA, PBS, or Sulapac) creates severe interfacial residual stress, causing the glass layer to fracture when the refill cup flexes during snap-fit insertion64.
To eliminate residual stress, an automated Radio-Frequency (RF) PECVD reactor rotates the vessel holders to deposit a functional bilayer or trilayer architecture on the interior surface61. The process begins by depositing a flexible plasma-polymerized organosilicon buffer/tie layer (  , containing    carbon normalized to total carbon plus oxygen plus silicon) directly onto the biopolymer wall or over a smoothing bio-lacquer primer to absorb mechanical strain61. Next, the precursor gas ratio is adjusted to deposit a pure, dense inorganic    barrier layer at an optimal thickness of just   61. Finally, for aqueous or high-pH cosmetic emulsions that could slowly etch nanoscale silica over a multi-year shelf life, a third pH-protective carbon-rich cap layer is deposited over the    layer61. Because the entire PECVD barrier stack is only nanometers thick, deposition times are exceptionally short for industrial scalability, container leachables are blocked from migrating into the cosmetic formula, and the coating adds virtually zero mass—preserving    certified industrial and home compostability61.


