The most critical engineering hurdle in manufacturing PHA lipstick mechanisms involves controlling post-molding secondary crystallization43. Because the glass transition temperature of PHA (  ) lies well below ambient room temperature (  ), the amorphous polymer chains in a newly molded lipstick cam sleeve retain high segmental mobility after ejection66. If an un-nucleated PHA part is quenched in a cold mold, it ejects in a partially amorphous, sticky state and subsequently undergoes slow secondary crystallization in the warehouse over 14 to 28 days (  ) at    and   20. During this two-to-four-week aging period, the polymer's modulus of elasticity climbs by    and the part undergoes    secondary volumetric shrinkage, warping thin cylindrical sleeves and jamming precision lipstick swivel tracks after assembly43.
